  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper proposes the Network Flow Compression Model, a graph neural network coarse-graining method that groups and merges nodes into compressed networks by minimizing partition function deviations across propagation scales, aiming to preserve macroscopic information-flow properties across different underlying topologies.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A graph-based compression proposal is supported when the paper identifies a flow-preservation limitation, defines a partition-function objective, implements node grouping with graph neural networks, and reports preservation and cross-domain compression behavior."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) Information flow on networks, such as signals or pathogens, is often studied through dynamical processes spanning short- to long-range interactions between components."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Because existing compression approaches are reported not to preserve macroscopic information dynamics at all scales and for all network types, flow-preserving compression is a plausible direction."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Minimizing deviations in the network partition function is used as a way to target preservation of macroscopic indicators derived from that partition function."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) A new flow-preserving network compression method is motivated by the reported limitations of existing approaches and by the partition-function preservation criterion."];
  "R25" [label="(0,0,0) Complex networks are used to represent heterogeneous natural and artificial systems and are modeled across fields such as biology, neuroscience, and social sciences."];
  "R30" [label="(0,0,0) Graph neural networks process and aggregate nodes' local features through message passing to capture graph dependencies."];
  "R31" [label="(0,0,0) Softmax-based node grouping together with group and adjacency matrices implements the network compression operation."];
  "R32" [label="(0,0,0) The approach seeks coarse-graining that preserves flow properties, with preservation across topology treated as the relevant criterion."];
  "R33" [label="(0,0,0) Numerical experiments, including partition-function mean absolute error at different sizes, report preservation of information flow under large compressions."];
  "R34" [label="(0,0,0) The paper reports that training graph neural networks on many real-world networks enables generalization to unseen datasets, with rapid and effective compressions across domains."];
